<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mithridatic Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/89-bce">89</a>–<a href="/years/63-bce">63 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mithridatic Wars were three wars fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> from 89 to 63 BCE. Mithridates overran Roman Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 88 BCE but was driven back by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, and later fought Murena, Lucullus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. After his final defeat he killed himself in 63 BCE, and Pontus was made a Roman province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithridatic-wars.jpg" alt="Mithridatic Wars" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Javierfv1212 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PonticKingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI Eupator</a> came to the throne of Pontus in a coup around 113 BCE, imprisoning his younger brother Chrestus and his mother Laodice. At first he looked north and east, accepting hegemony over parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and campaigning across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. His partition of Paphlagonia with Nicomedes III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> and his intervention in Cappadocia drew Roman envoys and a senatorial decree forcing him out of Cappadocia, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Lucius Cornelius Sulla</a> installed Ariobarzanes I as king.</p><p>An alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> strengthened Mithridates, and while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was distracted by the revolt of its Italian allies he again overthrew the ruler of Cappadocia. When Roman commissioners under Manius Aquillius pressed the restored kings to invade Pontus, he drove Ariobarzanes out once more and declared war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the first war Mithridates overran the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiatic-vespers">Asiatic Vespers</a>, the massacre of some 80,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Italians in the occupied territories, which caused a financial panic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Athens and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> went over to him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> landed, took Athens on 1 March 86 BCE and destroyed the Pontic forces at Chaeronea and Orchomenus. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-dardanos">Treaty of Dardanos</a> of 85 BCE forced Mithridates out of Roman Asia and Paphlagonia. It was lenient, probably because Sulla wanted a free hand against the regime at Rome. A second war began in 83 BCE when the governor Lucius Licinius Murena attacked Mithridates, and it ended inconclusively after Murena's defeat in 82 BCE.</p><p>The third war began in 73 BCE after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> on the death of its king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-iv">Nicomedes IV</a>. Lucius Licinius Lucullus relieved Cyzicus, drove Mithridates into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and invaded it, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranocerta">Tigranocerta</a> on 7 October 69 BCE, but his troops protested the length of the campaigns and he was replaced. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> took command in 66 BCE, defeated Mithridates, forced Tigranes of Armenia to submit, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Mithridates fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and killed himself when his son Pharnaces staged a coup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s settlement annexed Pontus, joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> as one province, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and set up client kingdoms on the Anatolian plateau, among them Cappadocia and Commagene. More than 36,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> were found in Mithridates' treasuries. Large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> came under Roman taxation for the first time, collected by publicani and enforced by garrisons.</p><p>The wars drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> far deeper into the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and shifted provincial policy permanently towards exploitation of the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s difficulty in winning ratification of his settlements and land for his veterans led him into an alliance with Marcus Crassus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mithridatic Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pompey the Great celebrated his third triumph, for his victories over the pirates and the end of the <b>Mithridatic Wars</b>, on his 45th birthday.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-29">29 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
